<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cosimo I de' Medici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1519">1519</a>–<a href="/years/1574">1574</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">House of Medici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cosimo I de' Medici became duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> at seventeen after the murder of his cousin Alessandro in 1537. He crushed the republican exiles at Montemurlo, conquered Siena in the 1550s with Spanish backing and built a centralised state, commissioning the Uffizi from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a>. In 1569 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> made him the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici.jpg" alt="Cosimo I de' Medici" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bronzino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agnolo_Bronzino_-_Cosimo_I_de%27_Medici_in_armour_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Alessandro was murdered by his cousin Lorenzino in January 1537, Florentine leaders chose <b>Cosimo de’ Medici</b>, a seventeen-year-old from a junior branch of the family.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Alessandro was murdered by his cousin Lorenzino in 1537, and the 17-year-old <b>Cosimo I de' Medici</b> was chosen to succeed him.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-florence">Duchy of Florence</a></li><li><blockquote>Its small size made it dependent on stronger powers: <b>Cosimo I de' Medici</b> built Portoferraio on Elba in 1548, and Spain later held Porto Longone.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-piombino">Principality of Piombino</a></li><li><blockquote>The Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis between France and Spain in 1559 withdrew French support, and Montalcino surrendered to <b>Cosimo I de' Medici</b>, joining the future Grand Duchy of Tuscany.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-siena-at-montalcino">Republic of Siena at Montalcino</a></li><li><blockquote>When Philip II of Spain granted the conquered Republic of Siena to <b>Cosimo I de' Medici</b> in 1557, he kept the coastal fortresses of Orbetello, Porto Ercole, Talamone, Porto Santo Stefano and Ansedonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-the-presidi">State of the Presidi</a></li></ul></section>
